   "source": [
    "### What I notice in the two auxiliary regressions\n",
    "\n",
    "**(a) `e` on `[1, ŷ]`** — `b = [1.1e-16, 2.8e-17]`, `R² = 0`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both coefficients are zero, and not as an empirical result — it is an algebraic identity.\n",
    "The normal equations give `X'e = 0`. Every regressor in this auxiliary regression lies in the\n",
    "column space of `X`: the constant is the first column of `X`, and `ŷ = Xb` is a linear\n",
    "combination of the columns of `X`. So `[1, ŷ]'e = 0` exactly, which *is* the normal equation\n",
    "of this auxiliary regression; its solution is therefore `b = (0, 0)`, its fitted values are\n",
    "all zero, and `R² = 0`. The printed `1e-16` values are floating-point noise — in exact\n",
    "arithmetic they vanish. Any `y` and any `X` containing a constant give the same result.\n",
    "\n",
    "Confirming numbers: `X'e = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]`, `ŷ'e ≈ 1.8e-15`, `Σe ≈ 1.8e-14`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**(b) `ŷ` on `[1, e]`** — `b = [0.0134, 3.5e-17]`, `R² = 0`.\n",
    "\n",
    "The slope is zero for the same reason, read backwards. The slope is\n",
    "`Σ(e - ē)(ŷ - ȳ) / Σ(e - ē)²`, where `ē = 0` (since `Σe = 0`) and `e'ŷ = 0` by the projection\n",
    "result, so the numerator vanishes. With a zero slope the intercept must equal `mean(ŷ)`,\n",
    "which is `0.0133946` — equal to `mean(y)` for the same `Σe = 0` reason. `R² = 0` is the same\n",
    "zero as in (a), since in a two-variable regression `R²` is the squared correlation and\n",
    "`corr(e, ŷ) = 0`, which is symmetric in the two variables.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Note — this is a demonstration, not a diagnostic.** It is tempting to read `R² = 0` as\n",
    "\"no misspecification\" or \"no heteroskedasticity\", but the result is guaranteed by construction\n",
    "for *any* data set. What it shows is that `P_X` and `M_X = I - P_X` are orthogonal projections\n",
    "with `M_X P_X = 0` — the same machinery question 5 asks you to manipulate in the partitioned\n",
    "regressions."
   ]
